To output a PDF in SAS in journal format, you can use the `ods` statement and specify the `pdf` destination and the `journal` style. Here's an example:
```sas
ods pdf file="output.pdf" style=journal;
  proc print data=sashelp.cars;
    title "Cars Dataset";
  run;
ods pdf close;
```
This code will generate a PDF file named "output.pdf" in the current working directory, with a journal-style layout. The `title` statement sets the title of the output.

You can customize the journal style further by using options such as `margin`, `font`, and `footer`. For example:
```sas
ods pdf file="output.pdf" style=journal(
  margin=(1in,1in,1in,1in) 
  font=symsql.8 
  footer=(text="Page &p of &n")
);
  proc print data=sashelp.cars;
    title "Cars Dataset";
  run;
ods pdf close;
```
This code will add a 1-inch margin on all sides, use the SymsQL font, and include a footer with the page number and total number of pages.

Note that the `style=journal` option is only available in SAS 9.2 and later versions. If you're using an earlier version, you may need to use the `ods destination` statement followed by the `pdf` destination and the `journal` style:
```sas
ods destination pdf;
  ods pdf file="output.pdf" style=journal;
    proc print data=sashelp.cars;
      title "Cars Dataset";
    run;
ods pdf close;
ods destination close;
```
Also, you can use `ods pdf path=` to specify the location where the pdf file will be saved.

